window.__hvBdRegister("julian-orations", {"key":"julian-orations","sections":[{"i":1,"j":12,"loc":"Oration I · Panegyric in Honour of Constantius 1–12","title":"Proem, plan of the speech, and ancestry","summary":"Julian explains his hesitation to praise Constantius: poets may invent and orators may flatter, but he, trained in other studies, will offer a plain narrative of facts. He cites the ancient maxim that every word and deed should aim at the beautiful and be referable to virtue and philosophy, and sets out his plan: ancestors, upbringing, achievements, and finally the personal virtues behind them, which he will stress most because exploits depend on fortune and troops. He leaves Rome's praise to the Emperor's own phrase, the teacher of virtue, then reviews the imperial family: Claudius, the two grandfathers and their harmony, the father who founded the city on the Bosporus and honoured Athens, and the mother who was daughter, wife, sister and mother of emperors.","ideas":["Panegyric has fallen under suspicion of flattery; Julian claims to give a factual record","Virtuous actions belong to the doer alone, while victories depend on fortune and soldiers","Many lands claim Constantius; Rome, the teacher of virtue, is preferred","The father's greatest achievement was begetting and educating Constantius"],"terms":[{"t":"epideictic oratory","d":"Display rhetoric of praise or blame, which Julian says he has not practised."},{"t":"panegyric","d":"A formal speech of public praise, here addressed to the reigning emperor."}]},{"i":13,"j":20,"loc":"Oration I 13–20","title":"The prince's education in body and mind","summary":"Julian dismisses birth portents as poetic licence and turns to Constantius' training. His physical exercise was military rather than athletic: running in armour, riding, archery, with little sleep and irregular food. His mind was trained by experience as well as study: following Plato's advice that boys should watch war, his father made him ruler of the Celts while young and let him learn strategy from barbarian feuds, then sent him to command against the Persians. Julian contrasts this with Spartan and Carthaginian training of rulers, praises his obedience to father, nature and law, and ends with his conduct at his father's death and toward brothers and friends.","ideas":["Military fitness, not athletes' condition, suits a king","Like Odysseus, a ruler must know many peoples and their ways","Spartans and Carthaginians failed to give future kings a special education","Learning to be governed is better than learning to govern"],"terms":[{"t":"hoplite","d":"A heavy-armed infantryman; Constantius is said to excel both as hoplite and horseman."}]},{"i":21,"j":29,"loc":"Oration I 21–29","title":"Accession, the Persian war, and Singara","summary":"Julian reviews Rome's long struggle with Persia and the crisis at Constantius' accession: a weak third of the empire, mutinous troops, and Armenians in revolt. He praises Constantius for conceding the larger share to his brothers for the sake of harmony, then for restoring order in Syria, reinstating loyal Armenians, training troops and funding the war without raising taxes. Raids across the Tigris always returned victorious. Julian then gives an account of the battle near Singara: the Persian king fled, Roman troops pursued against orders, took a fort, but fell to drinking at the cisterns; he argues the losses were equal and blames the heat, not the general.","ideas":["Giving up revenue to secure harmony was both honourable and expedient","Order returned even before Constantius arrived in Syria","Julian promises not to hide reverses, since falsehood is flattery","Skill is judged in storms, not fair weather: the pilot and charioteer analogies"],"terms":[{"t":"Singara","d":"Site of a battle against the Persians that Julian argues was a draw rather than a defeat."}]},{"i":30,"j":37,"loc":"Oration I 30–37","title":"Usurpation, the flooded siege, and the bloodless abdication","summary":"News comes that Galatia has gone to a usurper, Constantius' brother has been murdered, and the Illyrian garrisons have proclaimed their general emperor. While Constantius prepares to march west, the Persians besiege a Syrian city by damming the river Mygdonius and attacking with siege-engines on boats; the defence produces a slaughter in the water, and after four months the king retreats. Julian compares the siege favourably with Xerxes, Plataea, Syracuse and the Gauls at Rome. The Illyrian general, an old man who had pledged loyalty, joins the usurper; Constantius addresses both armies from a platform, wins them by speech alone, and receives the purple without bloodshed.","ideas":["A city ringed by water and attacked by ships: a siege without parallel","The Persian king outdid the Aloadae in folly yet failed","Constantius unites the roles of orator and general, surpassing Demosthenes' audiences","A victory won by persuasion is stainless, like a priest's act"],"terms":[{"t":"Thessalian persuasion","d":"Proverbial for persuasion backed by compulsion, used of the old general's surrender of the purple."}]},{"i":38,"j":43,"loc":"Oration I 38–43","title":"War against the usurper and the battle of Myrsa","summary":"Julian lists the usurper's crimes: a former slave who killed his master, executed officers, ruined discipline and imposed confiscatory taxes. His vast army of Celts, Galatians, Franks and Saxons stripped the Rhine of defence and terrified the whole empire. Constantius drew him out of the passes onto the plains before Myrsa, where his cavalry broke the enemy; the usurper fled while his troops fought on until driven into the river. Julian describes the new armoured cavalry in detail, the amnesty that followed, and the final campaign in which the usurper, hiding in a pleasure city in Italy, was surprised, fled to Galatia, grew crueller, and at last killed himself.","ideas":["The war was waged to avenge laws and citizens, not private wrongs","The usurper mistook a manoeuvre for flight and was caught in the open","The armoured horsemen look like glittering statues","The usurper's death by his own sword is called a fitting punishment"],"terms":[{"t":"cuirassiers","d":"Fully armoured cavalry, covered in mail from head to foot, which Julian credits Constantius with developing."}]},{"i":44,"j":51,"loc":"Oration I 44–51","title":"Benefactions and virtues: temperance, wisdom, clemency","summary":"Julian passes over further campaigns and recounts Constantius' benefactions, such as harbours for Antioch and walls for his ancestral city. He then tests his career against virtue: obedience to his father and harmony with his brothers, unlike Alexander and Cyrus; a just war undertaken from love of the noble rather than gain; generosity to cities and restoration of lost estates; and freedom from jealousy toward prosperous friends. He praises the choice of a colleague who shares the honour without the danger, the Emperor's temperance and chastity, and his wisdom. The section ends with the loyalty of the armies and his mercy after the end of another usurper in Galatia, including sparing the usurper's young son.","ideas":["A barbarian ruling Romans was the just cause of war","Good kings, unlike tyrants, do not envy their subjects' wealth or virtue","The surest defence of a ruler is his subjects' affection, won by imitating the divine","Clemency toward the guilty man's friends and child is the crown of virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"euboulia (wise counsel)","d":"The ability to find the measures that will prove good and expedient in practice."}]},{"i":52,"j":64,"loc":"Oration II · On Kingship 52–64","title":"Homeric preface: lineage, wealth, and arms","summary":"Julian opens with Achilles and Agamemnon: Homer warns kings against insolence and generals against resenting kings. Grateful for the Emperor's honours, Julian resolves to praise him, measuring him against Homer's heroes. He compares the dynasty from Claudius with the brief line of the Pelopids, then declines to dwell on horses, palaces and finery as frivolous. Turning to bodily strength and skill at arms, he sets the Emperor above Pandaros, Teucer and Meriones as archer and hunter, matches him with Achilles in armour, and claims his phalanx never broke as Menestheus' and Nestor's did.","ideas":["Homer's quarrel scene teaches restraint to both kings and generals","The imperial house outlasts and outranks the Pelopids","External splendour is left to the Homerids; virtue is the true theme","The Emperor combines skills each hero had singly"],"terms":[{"t":"Homerids","d":"Reciters and followers of Homer, to whom Julian leaves descriptions of luxury."}]},{"i":65,"j":84,"loc":"Oration II 65–84","title":"The battle by the river, compared with Achilles","summary":"Julian proposes to compare the Emperor's battles with those of Ajax, Achilles, Hector and Sarpedon, beginning with the recent battle by the river. A traitor who killed the Emperor's brother marched with Celts, Iberians and Germans, likened to Prodicus' figure of Vice; drawn into the plains of Paeonia, he was outflanked and fled. Julian describes the cavalry formation, the amnesty granted to suppliants, and the mysterious disappearance of the usurper's chief instigator. The enemy fought on in companies until routed into the Drave, a harsher river than the Scamander, whose fight with Achilles Julian treats as Homeric invention. Unlike Achilles' limited victory, this one left the usurper begging pardon behind mountain forts.","ideas":["The Emperor's mercy exceeds Achilles', who killed suppliants","The usurper's adviser vanished, perhaps snatched by an avenging power","Homer's partiality for Achilles distorts a fair judgment","Defeated troops kept fighting from pride, not from their leader's skill"],"terms":[{"t":"Prodicus' Vice","d":"The personified Vice in Prodicus' fable who tried to win Heracles from Virtue."}]},{"i":85,"j":96,"loc":"Oration II 85–96","title":"The siege by the Mygdonius and Hector's failings","summary":"Julian compares Ajax's defence of the ships with the siege of the city on the Mygdonius. The Parthian king flooded the plain, attacked with ships and engines, and when the wall collapsed sent armoured cavalry and Indian elephants carrying towers; both sank in the bog before the moat and were repulsed. Overnight the defenders raised a second wall, and the king withdrew after heavy losses, executing his satraps. Julian notes that the Greeks abandoned their wall to Hector while this garrison held, then criticises Hector for cowardice and for leaving battle to carry a message about rites he should have performed himself.","ideas":["Parthians imitate Persian dress to claim an old empire","Military arms are valued according to terrain, as with Parthian cavalry","Julian defends his digressions against the rule-keepers of panegyric","A king should serve the gods in person, like a priest"],"terms":[{"t":"Parthians","d":"Julian's name for the Persians of his day, whom he says pretend to be Persians."}]},{"i":97,"j":106,"loc":"Oration II 97–106","title":"The god within; the Alpine fort taken","summary":"Julian paraphrases Plato: the best-equipped man depends on himself, and his real self is his mind, the divine genius within, not his body or property. He argues that one may substitute God for genius, that kings should yield the reins of life to God, and that piety, the child of justice, requires traditional worship. Returning to Hector, he compares the Achaean wall with an ancient Alpine fort the usurper garrisoned while staying in a rich coastal city of the Heneti; he describes the Alps and Italy's borders. The Emperor took the position without losing a man, surpassing Alexander at the Indian rock, and his many wars outnumber the single Trojan war.","ideas":["A man's possessions are not himself; his intellect is","Pure intelligence unmixed with matter is synonymous with God","Plato's doctrines season speech like salt and gold dust","Swap the Emperor's name for the heroes' and the Iliad fits him"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn (genius)","d":"Plato's guiding spirit within each person, identified with the highest part of the soul."},{"t":"eusebeia (piety)","d":"Scrupulous devotion to the gods, called the child of justice."}]},{"i":107,"j":114,"loc":"Oration II 107–114","title":"Eloquence, the Illyrian assembly, and Socrates' standard","summary":"Julian compares the Emperor's counsel and speech with Nestor and Odysseus, whose persuasion failed with Achilles and whose wall made the Achaeans careless. He recalls the assembly in Illyria where the Emperor, unarmed and unguarded, won over the rival armies by a dignified, simple speech, and the old usurper handed over the purple and was pensioned off. Julian declines to reproduce the Emperor's words. He then raises Socrates, who praised not conquest or empire but only those who joined courage with temperance and wisdom with justice, and states the wise men's view that virtue makes the soul kingly and truly wealthy.","ideas":["A tearless victory even for the defeated","The Emperor's style suits both the learned and the unlearned","Popular eulogists merely echo their audience's opinions","Virtue keeps a man safe in shipwreck, crowd or tyranny"],"terms":[{"t":"aretē (virtue)","d":"The excellence of soul that, on the wise men's account, alone makes one happy, kingly and wealthy."}]},{"i":115,"j":115,"loc":"Oration II 115","title":"Virtue as true wealth and noble birth","summary":"Julian likens virtue to the sun's light, which cannot be stolen or diminished by being shared. Its possessor alone is wealthy, well-born and a king. Noble birth, he argues, is judged by the soul, not inherited money: the ancients called virtuous men sons of Zeus, while a bad son of good parents is a bastard. No wealth, purple, armies or acclamation makes a king; rulers who lack wisdom are not even free but slaves to pleasure and money, like Phaethon raised to ruin. Lovers of money suffer a lifelong hunger, as Darius did in robbing tombs.","ideas":["The sun loses no light by giving it; neither does the good man","Signs of lineage are written on the soul","Power without virtue harms receiver and givers alike","Natural needs are limited; greed is endless"],"terms":[{"t":"eugeneia (noble birth)","d":"For Julian, likeness to virtuous parents or virtue of one's own, not inherited riches."}]},{"i":116,"j":117,"loc":"Oration II 116–117","title":"Portrait of the true king","summary":"Checking his invective, Julian describes the good king: devout, dutiful to parents, kind to strangers, rich in friends' goodwill, brave but averse to war and civil strife, and merciful after victory. He shepherds citizens and trains soldiers like guard dogs, leading by example and keeping them supplied. He guards and frames laws, treats his fatherland as more sacred than kin, cures curable offenders, and leaves capital cases to a sober court chosen by lot, keeping no sword in his own hand. Imitating God, who made only good and appoints judges and avengers of evil, he chooses officials by careful testing. His people live in peace and the gods reward him.","ideas":["Soldiers should be guardians, not wolves among the flock","Law is the child of justice and adjunct of the highest god","Two kinds of wrongdoers: curable and incurable","The king is God's prophet and vice-regent, without a sting like the queen-bee"],"terms":[{"t":"basileus (king)","d":"The true ruler, guided by the kingly part of the soul rather than the emotional."},{"t":"daimones","d":"Divine agents who protect, judge, or carry out punishment on wrongdoers."}]},{"i":118,"j":125,"loc":"Oration II 118–125","title":"The Emperor measured: father, brothers, just war","summary":"Julian restates his rule: praise should look to character, not gifts of fortune, and kings are happy as they approach the model just drawn. Unlike rhetoricians who praise royal descent, he asks how advantages were used. The Emperor's father loved him for his character, gave him the preferred region and summoned him alone at death; he dealt justly with his brothers and willingly gave up territory. His war against the usurper had a juster cause than Troy or Alexander's, since the usurper was a recent captive, and after victory he spared even slanderers. Julian praises his restraint under calumny, a passion Socrates alone mastered.","ideas":["Property is good only when well used","Ruling more nations needs no different virtue, only more care","Slander wounds the noblest souls most, through love of glory","Bearing calumny calmly rivals taking Troy"],"terms":[{"t":"philotimia (love of glory)","d":"The last garment the soul puts off, which makes great men resent slander."}]},{"i":126,"j":127,"loc":"Oration II 126–127","title":"Loyalty of friends and the spared child","summary":"The Emperor's friends and even enemies trust him: leading senators fled to him in Paeonia and a cavalry division defected before the battle on the Drave, while he stayed calm after a minor setback like a pilot in a storm. A commander in Galatia, who had left his son as hostage, bribed barbarians and donned a purple robe, and was torn apart by his own soldiers. The Emperor spared the son and the man's friends unless proven guilty, unlike Agamemnon, who wished to destroy even the unborn. Julian argues this mercy proves justice, restraint, courage and wisdom, and closes by saying he must now turn to his work.","ideas":["Fortune deceives fools with small wins and steadies the wise","The soldiers' loyalty repaid a just rule","Sweet fruit grows from bitter wood: sons need not share fathers' guilt","The Emperor's endurance and chastity are left unrecounted"],"terms":[]},{"i":128,"j":139,"loc":"Oration III · Panegyric in Honour of the Empress Eusebia (1 of 5)","title":"Why gratitude demands praise of a woman","summary":"Julian opens by condemning ingratitude, including the silence of those who hide benefits received for fear of seeming flatterers. He argues that praise benefits both the one praised and those spurred to imitate them, and that Socrates, Plato, Aristotle and Xenophon all wrote eulogies. Since virtue belongs to women as much as to men, a noble woman deserves an encomium too, and accepting help from a woman is no disgrace. Homer's Athene, guiding Odysseus to Queen Arete and praising her lineage and judgement, becomes his model, and he resolves to follow her order: country, ancestors, marriage and the rest.","ideas":["Concealing a kindness is itself a form of ingratitude","Public praise, unlike public gift-giving, is honourable","Excellence belongs to women no less than to men","Athene's praise of Arete sets the pattern for the speech"],"terms":[{"t":"enkōmion (encomium)","d":"A formal speech of praise, here owed as repayment of a benefit."}]},{"i":140,"j":143,"loc":"Oration III · Panegyric in Honour of the Empress Eusebia (2 of 5)","title":"Eusebia's homeland, father, and marriage","summary":"Julian passes over Macedonia's mythic past but recalls its settlement by the Heraclid sons of Temenus and the conquests of Philip and of Alexander, pupil of the Stagyrite. Eusebia's father held the consulship, an office that keeps its prestige under monarchy, and Julian argues that founding a family's distinction is nobler than inheriting it. The Emperor, after recovering his throne from a usurper, chose her for her birth, wisdom, youth and beauty, after long inquiry. Her mother, of pure Greek stock from Macedonia's capital, surpassed Evadne and Laodameia in self-control by devoting herself to her children after widowhood.","ideas":["Macedonia's fame rests on Philip and Alexander","The consulship is still prized by citizens and emperors alike","To found a line's glory is nobler than to inherit it","The Emperor chose Eusebia for all her gifts together, not beauty alone"],"terms":[{"t":"consul","d":"The annual office that once held royal power and now survives as the highest honour."}]},{"i":144,"j":145,"loc":"Oration III · Panegyric in Honour of the Empress Eusebia (3 of 5)","title":"Eusebia as a second Penelope","summary":"Julian refuses to describe the bridal procession and costly gifts, comparing such detail to admiring a musician's robe rather than his playing. Penelope's chief merit was that Odysseus preferred her to Calypso, Circe and Nausicaa and made her the partner of his counsels. Eusebia, he claims, draws equally on love and on the affection virtue inspires, and so shares her husband's counsels and turns his justice toward mercy. Where Athenian law acquitted on an equal vote, she pleads for acquittal even when the votes are nearly equal, and no one can blame her for any punishment.","ideas":["Outward splendour is not a fit subject for true praise","Odysseus chose Penelope over goddesses and luxury","Eusebia persuades the Emperor to mercy and acquittal","She has caused no one's misfortune and many benefits"],"terms":[{"t":"vote of Athene","d":"The Athenian rule that a tied jury acquits the defendant."}]},{"i":146,"j":151,"loc":"Oration III · Panegyric in Honour of the Empress Eusebia (4 of 5)","title":"Eusebia's kindness to Julian himself","summary":"Eusebia honoured her kin and her father's friends by merit, then Julian offers himself as witness, swearing he speaks without flattery since he already has what a flatterer would seek. When the Emperor grew suspicious of him, she secured him an audience, his acquittal, safe conduct home, and then leave to visit Greece, where philosophy still flows like the Nile's unfailing springs. She urged him to accept the honour the Emperor pressed on him; Julian compares himself to an unskilled driver handed a master charioteer's team of restive horses. At their first interview she seemed to him a statue of Modesty, and spoke only a few gentle words.","ideas":["Like Darius repaying the Samian's cloak, Julian repays a debt","Eusebia rescued him from slander and sent him to Greece","Philosophy has not wholly left Greece","Accepting high office felt like driving another's unruly team"],"terms":[{"t":"philosophos (philosopher)","d":"A name Julian says he bears without the reality, though Eusebia honoured even the name."}]},{"i":152,"j":157,"loc":"Oration III · Panegyric in Honour of the Empress Eusebia (5 of 5)","title":"Gift of books and the praise of mercy","summary":"Among her gifts Julian singles out a library of philosophy, history, oratory and poetry, which made Gaul a Greek temple of the Muses; he carries such histories even on campaign, as they give the young the experience of age and patterns for character. A grateful recipient must repay such a gift, as a farmer or herdsman should share his yield. Against sophists who praise only marvels like Semiramis, he holds that Homer praised Penelope's discretion because it brought benefit, not calamity. Citing Pericles, who prided himself on causing no citizen's death, Julian makes Eusebia's beneficent power the sum of his encomium, declining to dwell on the splendour of her visit to Rome.","ideas":["Histories train character by noble examples","Benefits received ought to be shared and acknowledged","Quiet virtue outranks spectacular deeds","Saving citizens from harm is the greatest proof of virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"sophists","d":"Professional orators whom Julian depicts as craving marvellous themes."}]},{"i":158,"j":163,"loc":"Oration IV · Hymn to King Helios (1 of 5)","title":"Julian's devotion and the three suns","summary":"Julian declares himself a follower of King Helios, drawn to the sun and the night sky from childhood, and calls the god the common father of mankind. He sets out to praise the god's substance, origin, powers and gifts for the festival Rome celebrates. The eternal universe is guarded by the Fifth Substance, by the intelligible world, and above all by the One or Good, which produced Helios as its likeness among the intellectual gods, as in Plato's analogy of the sun and the Good. The visible disc is third in rank; light is the incorporeal activity of the transparent, making sight and visibility perfect, and the sun governs the planets and the phases of the moon.","ideas":["Helios is the offspring and likeness of the Good","Three levels: intelligible, intellectual, and visible sun","Light is incorporeal, the activity of mind","Planets dance about the sun as their king"],"terms":[{"t":"Fifth Substance","d":"The heavenly element whose culmination is the sun's rays."},{"t":"intellectual gods","d":"The middle order of gods whom Helios rules, between the intelligible and visible gods."}]},{"i":164,"j":170,"loc":"Oration IV · Hymn to King Helios (2 of 5)","title":"Helios as the middle of all things","summary":"Julian lists five powers of Helios: perfecting, creating, unifying, his middle station, and kingship among the intellectual gods. An oracle of Apollo identifying Zeus, Hades and Helios Serapis shows a single sovereignty, and Hades is a mild god who lifts souls to the intelligible; Hesiod's Hyperion and Homer's Zeus confirm the sun's freedom and perfecting power. Helios keeps becoming alive by approach and withdrawal. His middleness is not a mean between opposites but a unifying link, and he stands between the intelligible and visible worlds in unity, cohesion, creation, generative life, and purity. He ends with a summary of the god's substance, including the creation of the sun's angels.","ideas":["Zeus, Hades and Helios share one sovereignty","Hades frees souls from generation rather than punishing them","Middleness means linking, as Empedocles' Harmony","Helios mirrors the intelligible in each perfection of the visible"],"terms":[{"t":"mesotēs (middleness)","d":"The unifying position that joins extremes, not a mere average between opposites."},{"t":"Hyperion","d":"Helios's father in Hesiod, read as the being above all things."}]},{"i":171,"j":176,"loc":"Oration IV · Hymn to King Helios (3 of 5)","title":"The supra-mundane powers of Helios","summary":"In a god, substance, power and activity are one, unlike in humans, whose soul and body are at war. Helios binds the extremes of the intellectual world as air and water link fire and earth. He shares creative rule with Zeus, singleness of thought with Apollo, divided creation with Dionysus, and is himself leader of the Muses and father of Asclepios. His works include distributing the Good's blessedness and intelligible beauty to the intellectual gods, giving them thought as light gives sight, and endowing angels, daemons, heroes and unembodied souls with a higher lot.","ideas":["In gods, will, power and act coincide","Helios is linked with Zeus, Apollo, Dionysus and Asclepios","Intelligible beauty guides generation in this world","Higher races receive their nobler lot from Helios"],"terms":[{"t":"dēmiourgia (creative function)","d":"The making activity Helios shares with Zeus and, in divided form, with Dionysus."}]},{"i":177,"j":180,"loc":"Oration IV · Hymn to King Helios (4 of 5)","title":"Visible creation: spheres, Athene, Aphrodite","summary":"Following Iamblichus, Julian rejects even a hypothetical creation in time: Helios brought all things forth from eternity and holds the mid-heavens, guiding the seven spheres, the eighth, and our world. He interprets the Graces, the Dioscuri, and Oceanus astronomically, notes the mysteries' teaching that the sun moves beyond the fixed stars, and describes the zodiac's division into thirty-six gods. Athene Pronoia came whole from Helios as his perfect intelligence, bringing wisdom down to the Moon and the arts to mankind. Aphrodite assists his creation as the love that unites the heavenly gods; at Emesa, Monimos and Azizos are Hermes and Ares.","ideas":["The world has no beginning in time","Twelve zodiac powers divided into thirty-six gods","Athene Pronoia is Helios's intelligence","Aphrodite is the bond of the heavens and joint cause of birth"],"terms":[{"t":"Athene Pronoia","d":"Athene as Forethought, said here to proceed whole from Helios."},{"t":"Iamblichus","d":"Julian's acknowledged source for this theology."}]},{"i":181,"j":188,"loc":"Oration IV · Hymn to King Helios (5 of 5)","title":"Gifts to mankind, Rome, and prayer","summary":"Below the moon Helios guides the elements, judges and purifies souls, gives nature generative power, and makes weather. To mankind he gives number through day and night, Dionysus and the Muses, Apollo's oracles and Greek colonies, Asclepios, and light. Rome is his city: Aeneas descends from Aphrodite, Quirinus's soul came from Helios, Numa set up the Vestal flame and a solar year, and the new year and the Invincible Sun's games follow the winter solstice. Julian prays for Rome and himself, then dedicates the hymn, composed in three nights, to Sallust and credits Iamblichus as his source.","ideas":["Helios is the source of every blessing in human life","Roman religion and constitution are Greek","Rome's founding and calendar honour the sun","A hymn is a sacrifice offered according to one's powers"],"terms":[{"t":"Heliaia","d":"The games of the Invincible Sun held after the Kronia."},{"t":"lycabas","d":"A name for the year that Julian links with the wolf."}]},{"i":189,"j":192,"loc":"Oration V · Hymn to the Mother of the Gods (1 of 4)","title":"The cult's arrival and who Attis is","summary":"Julian recounts how Athens, after insulting Gallus, built the Metroum on Apollo's advice, and how Rome brought the goddess's image from Phrygia during the Carthaginian war. The ship stuck fast in the Tiber until the maiden Claudia, falsely suspected, towed it upstream with her girdle, proving the image living and the goddess all-seeing. He defends the story against sceptics. Attis or Gallus, he explains, is the generative Mind of the third creator, holding the causes of forms embodied in matter. He rejects the Peripatetic Xenarchus's view that the Fifth Substance suffices as cause, insisting that Aristotle must be harmonized with Plato and the oracles.","ideas":["Claudia's miracle vindicated her and the goddess","City traditions deserve more trust than clever doubters","Attis is the lowest creative cause, reaching down to matter","Matter and form need a higher cause, or Epicurean chance follows"],"terms":[{"t":"Attis / Gallus","d":"The god whom Julian reads as the generative cause of material forms."},{"t":"Metroum","d":"The Athenian temple of the Mother, also used as the state archive."}]},{"i":193,"j":195,"loc":"Oration V · Hymn to the Mother of the Gods (2 of 4)","title":"Forms, the myth of Attis, and castration","summary":"Julian argues that the soul, Aristotle's place of the forms, holds them potentially like a mirror, so they must exist actually in immaterial causes above. Attis, exposed by the river Gallus (the Milky Way) and crowned with stars, was loved by the Mother but descended into the cave and wedded the nymph, the dampness before matter. The Mother, source of the intellectual gods and consort of Zeus, wanted him to turn toward her, since turning upward is more powerful than inclining downward. Helios persuaded the fiery Lion to expose him, and the castration means the checking of the unlimited; Attis is then led back up.","ideas":["Forms exist actually before they exist potentially in soul","The myth's places and figures map onto the cosmos","Conversion to the higher is more effective than descent","Castration signifies limiting unlimited generation"],"terms":[{"t":"place of the forms","d":"Aristotle's description of the soul, which holds forms potentially."},{"t":"Mother of the Gods","d":"Source of the intellectual gods and Providence over generation."}]},{"i":196,"j":202,"loc":"Oration V · Hymn to the Mother of the Gods (3 of 4)","title":"The festival's rites and seasons explained","summary":"The sacred tree is felled at the spring equinox, then come trumpets, the castration, and the Hilaria; the tree teaches us to offer virtue and piety and strive upward. The trumpet recalls Attis and us to the limited and the One. Julian explains that the ancients clothed truths in paradoxical myths so the absurdity would prompt a search, and that nothing in the myth means the gods err: Attis forever descends and forever is limited. The rising sun's uplifting rays explain the honour paid to that season, and the Mysteries of Deo and the chastity of the hierophant are likewise tied to equinox and limit.","ideas":["Each rite symbolizes the soul's return upward","Myths are made paradoxical to provoke inquiry","The story describes an eternal process, not events in time","Sunlight lifts souls as it draws up bodies"],"terms":[{"t":"Hilaria","d":"The joyful festival following the castration, signifying the soul's ascent."},{"t":"hierophant","d":"The Eleusinian leader who renounces generation."}]},{"i":203,"j":208,"loc":"Oration V · Hymn to the Mother of the Gods (4 of 4)","title":"Rules of purification and closing hymn","summary":"Julian takes up the ritual's food rules, which many mock, and thanks the Mother for rescuing him from his own darkness. Restating that purification aims at the soul's ascent, he explains that roots, pomegranates, and fish are banned as belonging to the earth and underworld, apples and dates as sacred, and the pig as wholly earthbound, while upward-growing plants, birds and most animals are allowed. Obeying the ordinances lets divine light fill the soul, which strengthens the breath and heals the body. Composed in one night, the oration ends by linking the Mother with Athene, Dionysus and Hermes, and with a prayer for mankind, Rome, and Julian's own perfection in theurgy.","ideas":["Forbidden foods are those tending downward","Fish are not tended or offered in the highest sacrifices","Purification benefits body as well as soul","Attis as Logos is mad in descent, wise once limited"],"terms":[{"t":"hagneia (rite of purification)","d":"The ritual abstinence whose aim is the soul's ascent."},{"t":"theurgy","d":"Ritual work with the gods, in which Julian prays to be made perfect."}]},{"i":209,"j":216,"loc":"Oration VI · To the Uneducated Cynics (1 of 4)","title":"A soft Cynic, and philosophy as one","summary":"Julian opens by mocking a self-styled Cynic who shuns cold baths, calls Diogenes conceited for eating raw octopus, and treats death as an evil, unlike Socrates and Diogenes. He announces that he will set out what his teachers taught him about Cynicism, a branch of philosophy rivalling the noblest. Philosophy, the gift of reason sent through Prometheus and Hermes, may be defined as the art of arts, as becoming like God, or as \"Know thyself\"; these definitions converge, since self-knowledge embraces soul, body and the divine in us, and the gods excel us in knowledge. Truth and philosophy are one, reached by many roads, and the Stoics too took self-knowledge as their starting point.","ideas":["The Cynic who fears cold and death has abandoned Diogenes' example","Self-knowledge contains knowledge of body, soul, the divine and all the arts","Becoming like God means acquiring knowledge, in which the gods surpass us","Philosophy is one, like the roads to Athens: different routes, one goal"],"terms":[{"t":"gnōthi seauton (Know thyself)","d":"The Delphic precept Julian treats as the first principle of every philosophy, Cynicism included."},{"t":"homoiōsis theōi (likeness to God)","d":"The aim of becoming like God as far as possible, which Julian equates with knowing the nature of things."}]},{"i":217,"j":223,"loc":"Oration VI · To the Uneducated Cynics (2 of 4)","title":"Apollo as founder, Diogenes and raw meat","summary":"Since the Cynics left no serious treatises (the tragedies of Diogenes are ascribed to Philiscus), Cynicism must be judged by its practice, not its jests; like the Silenus figures of the Symposium, it hides gods within. Its founder is neither Antisthenes nor Diogenes nor even Heracles but the Pythian god, who gave Diogenes the command to \"falsify the common currency\" along with \"Know thyself\". Plato and Diogenes shared one aim, scorn of popular opinion and love of truth, though Plato worked through words and Diogenes through deeds. Julian then defends Diogenes' eating of raw octopus as a test of whether his disgust came from reason or mere custom, turning the charge back on his opponent, who eats pickled foods and whom he calls an omnivorous Egyptian.","ideas":["Cynicism is judged by its deeds, not by frivolous writings","Apollo is the true founder of Cynic philosophy","Plato and Diogenes pursued the same end by different means","Raw octopus was Diogenes' trial of custom against reason"],"terms":[{"t":"paracharattein to nomisma (falsify the common currency)","d":"The oracle given to Diogenes, read by Julian as a command to reject the coinage of conventional opinion."},{"t":"apatheia (freedom from emotion)","d":"The Cynic goal, which Julian says is equivalent to becoming a god."}]},{"i":224,"j":228,"loc":"Oration VI · To the Uneducated Cynics (3 of 4)","title":"Happiness, freedom, and the true Cynic's piety","summary":"The end of Cynicism, as of all philosophy, is happiness, understood as living according to nature; for man that means happiness resides in the mind, the gift Zeus gave us in the myth of the Protagoras. Diogenes, homeless and penniless, lived more happily than the Great King by hardening his body and following reason. True freedom is not escaping a purchase price but being ruled neither by belly nor by the opinions of the many; one must first tame the many-headed beast of anger and appetite before defying opinion, as Diogenes taught by striking a youth who flaunted his scorn of convention. Julian contrasts the world's slanders against philosophers with the plain living praised by Diogenes and Crates, insists that the Cynic must be reverent like Diogenes and not shameless like Oenomaus, and quotes Crates' hymn to the Muses.","ideas":["Human happiness lies in the mind, the best part of us","A man is a slave to whoever can punish or please him, including his appetites","Passion must be mastered before opinion can safely be scorned","Diogenes honoured the gods with his soul, not with offerings"],"terms":[{"t":"kata physin (according to nature)","d":"The standard of happiness, by which plants, animals, gods and men each flourish in their own way."},{"t":"eleutheria (freedom)","d":"For Julian, independence from bodily desire and from the opinions of the many, not merely legal status."}]},{"i":229,"j":233,"loc":"Oration VI · To the Uneducated Cynics (4 of 4)","title":"Advice to would-be Cynics; Diogenes defended","summary":"Julian tells the aspiring Cynic to cross-examine himself about his love of food, comfort and fame, and to withdraw like a weak bull until he has gained strength; reason and a plan of life, not staff, wallet and long hair, are the marks of the Cynic. Free speech must be earned, as it was by Crates, who gave away his property, laughed at his own deformity and gently reconciled quarrelling friends. Diogenes' shocking public acts were aimed at men who hide natural functions yet practise robbery and false accusation openly. Julian reproaches his opponent for pitying a man whom the Greeks admired next to Socrates and Pythagoras and whom Alexander admired, and closes by noting he wrote the speech in two days.","ideas":["Self-examination and training must precede Cynic frankness","Reason, not the costume, is the Cynic's mintmark","Diogenes' indecencies rebuked worse but concealed vices","Modern Cynics imitate only what is easiest in Diogenes"],"terms":[{"t":"parrhēsia (freedom of speech)","d":"Cynic frankness, which Julian says one may use only after proving one's worth."}]},{"i":234,"j":238,"loc":"Oration VII · To the Cynic Heracleios (1 of 6)","title":"A blasphemous Cynic and the origin of myths","summary":"Julian recounts sitting through a Cynic's lecture that told a myth profaning the highest of the gods, and states his plan: to show that a Cynic should write discourses rather than myths, what kind of myths philosophy may use, and how to revere the gods. Myth was probably first invented by pastoral peoples and suits the human soul, which yearns for knowledge while still imprisoned; myths were written for childish souls, like leather toys for teething infants. Poets developed the moral fable for adults, as Hesiod and Archilochus did, and Aesop, a slave by birth, used fables because he lacked freedom of speech. Since the Cynic claims to be the only free man, Julian asks what need he has of fables or of borrowing divine names such as Helios, Pan and Zeus for men.","ideas":["Myths were made to soothe souls not yet able to learn the truth","The fable is a myth that carries moral exhortation for adults","Aesop used fable because as a slave he could not speak openly","A free Cynic has no reason to disguise men under gods' names"],"terms":[{"t":"mythos (myth)","d":"Fictional narrative, which Julian traces to pastoral peoples and to the needs of immature souls."},{"t":"ainos (moral fable)","d":"The poets' development of myth, aimed at men and carrying hidden moral instruction."}]},{"i":239,"j":245,"loc":"Oration VII · To the Cynic Heracleios (2 of 6)","title":"Oenomaus rejected; the piety of Diogenes and Crates","summary":"Julian denies that myth-telling was ever a Cynic custom, and condemns Oenomaus, whose writings against the oracles aim to destroy reverence for the gods and the law against taking others' property; such Cynics are worse than pirates because they subvert society openly. The tragedies attributed to Diogenes or Philiscus, and those of Oenomaus, are infamous and cannot show the Cynic character. Diogenes must instead be judged by his deeds: he obeyed the god's twin commands, went to Olympia to honour the god, stayed in Corinth as sent there by the gods, while Crates' hymn shows his piety. The true Cynics taught by example before words, honoured piety above costly sacrifice, and had no enemies, since no one could injure them.","ideas":["Oenomaus's Cynicism attacks the gods and the moral law","Self-knowledge is the truth by which the common currency is restamped","Diogenes' journey to Olympia and stay in Corinth show reverence","Piety, not hecatombs, is the offering the gods value"],"terms":[{"t":"eusebeia (piety)","d":"Reverence for the gods, which Julian, quoting Euripides, calls queen of the gods and worth more than any sacrifice."}]},{"i":246,"j":255,"loc":"Oration VII · To the Cynic Heracleios (3 of 6)","title":"Where myths belong; Heracles and Dionysus allegorised","summary":"Julian divides philosophy into natural (theology, mathematics, physics), practical (ethics, economics, politics) and logic (demonstrative, polemic, eristic), and assigns myth only to ethics for individuals and to the theology of the Mysteries, since nature loves to hide divine truth from the profane. Following one he reveres next to the gods, he argues that the incongruous element in sacred myths summons us past their literal sense toward the hidden truth, before stopping short of what is too sacred to say about Dionysus. Diction about the gods must be wholly dignified, while the thought may be paradoxical. He reads Heracles' feats as acts of a divine body subject to pure intelligence, and interprets Semele as a prophetess who foretold Dionysus' manifestation, the god who brought the vine and civilised life; those without Dionysiac frenzy risk the fate of Pentheus.","ideas":["Myth suits only individual ethics and mystical theology","Paradox in sacred myth signals a hidden meaning","Language about gods must be reverent even when the thought is strange","Heracles and Dionysus were divine manifestations, not deified men"],"terms":[{"t":"teletē (initiation into the Mysteries)","d":"The branch of theology whose myths, like those of Orpheus, conceal truth in riddles."},{"t":"to aprosphoron (the incongruous)","d":"The strange or unfitting element in a myth that compels the reader to seek its hidden sense."}]},{"i":256,"j":259,"loc":"Oration VII · To the Cynic Heracleios (4 of 6)","title":"Rebuke of the Cynics and the shortcut","summary":"Julian asks what Heracleios has ever achieved to merit Diogenes' staff and frankness, and why he visited Constantius but not Gaul. He compares the wandering Cynics to the Galilaeans' monks, who gain honour and \"alms\" through small sacrifices, and complains of the Cynics who crowded his headquarters and courted his secretary instead of the schools. Ignorant rhetoricians rush into Cynicism thinking it a short road to virtue, but in philosophy beginning and end are one: knowing oneself and becoming like the gods. The true Cynic judges gold and sand alike as earth, satisfies bodily needs with what comes to hand, and recognises that he is divine; Julian then returns to myth, noting that Heracleios's myth was merely an old one adapted.","ideas":["Today's Cynics resemble Christian monks in their wandering and gain","The short road to virtue is harder than the long one","The true shortcut is to recognise one's divinity and despise the body","Heracleios did not even invent his own myth"],"terms":[{"t":"syntomos hodos (short road)","d":"The Cynics' claimed quick way to virtue, which Julian says only works for those able to go the long way."}]},{"i":260,"j":264,"loc":"Oration VII · To the Cynic Heracleios (5 of 6)","title":"Julian's myth of the rich man's heirs","summary":"Julian tells his own myth: a rich man who neglected the gods divided his wealth among sons whom he never taught virtue, and they slaughtered one another, demolished the ancestral temples and built sepulchres. Zeus, pitying the house, consults the Fates and charges Helios to rear a despised nephew in whom a spark of the god survives, with Athene's help. Guided by Hermes, the youth reaches a mountain, sees Helios in a trance, and begs to stay, but Helios and Athene send him back to govern in place of the sleeping heir, whose shepherds plunder the flock. They warn him against flatterers, bid him treat friends as friends and love his subjects, and arm him with a torch, aegis, helmet and Hermes' golden wand, promising that he will become a god if he follows them.","ideas":["Impiety and an untaught inheritance lead to fratricide and ruined temples","Helios rescues a neglected kinsman bearing his spark","The youth is sent back to cleanse his ancestral house","Rule must rest on piety, loyalty to friends and care for subjects"],"terms":[{"t":"Hēlios Basileus (King Helios)","d":"The sun god who, in Julian's myth, rears the youth and charges him with rule."}]},{"i":265,"j":269,"loc":"Oration VII · To the Cynic Heracleios (6 of 6)","title":"Reverence for divine names; Diogenes uninitiated","summary":"Julian mocks Heracleios for casting himself as Zeus and Julian as Pan, recalling Salmoneus's punishment, and contrasts his uneducated insolence with his own training under teachers who made virtue and the gods his guides; he went the long way round and stands at least at the entrance to virtue. He rebukes the empty ornament of bad rhetoricians and cites Pythagoras, Plato in the Philebus and Timaeus, and Aristotle for their reverence toward the gods and their names. He explains that Diogenes refused initiation not from impiety but to avoid becoming an Athenian citizen bound to one city's laws, being a citizen of the world, and that his retort was meant to reform a man who trusted in initiation without a good life. The oration breaks off with a question about where the discourse would end.","ideas":["Mortals who take divine names are punished, like Salmoneus","Pythagoras, Plato and Aristotle revered the names of the gods","Diogenes avoided Athenian citizenship, not the Mysteries","The gods reward right living above the ceremony of initiation"],"terms":[{"t":"kosmopolitēs (citizen of the world)","d":"Diogenes' self-description, which Julian uses to explain his refusal of Athenian initiation."}]},{"i":270,"j":276,"loc":"Oration VIII · A Consolation upon the Departure of Sallust (1 of 2)","title":"Grief at Sallust's departure; Scipio and Laelius","summary":"Julian addresses Sallust, his comrade in public and private affairs, who has been ordered away from him, and seeks a remedy for his grief. A sound mind, he says, can draw sweetness from hardship as the bee does from bitter herbs. He recalls their shared labours, candid talk and opposition to evildoers, and notes that sycophants aimed at him through Sallust; quoting Plato on the scarcity of loyal fellow-workers, he laments losing his frank counsellor. Choosing consolation from history seasoned with philosophy, he cites Scipio and Laelius, whose partnership was mocked, Pythagoras' maxim that friends have all things in common, and the many great men who parted from friends, introducing Pericles' separation from Anaxagoras.","ideas":["Friendship built on virtue and shared purpose, not oaths","Good government needs loyal friends, who are rare","Friends hold counsel and credit in common","Great men bore separation from friends without despair"],"terms":[{"t":"koina ta philōn (friends have all things in common)","d":"Pythagoras' maxim, which Julian extends to shared wisdom and shared credit for deeds."}]},{"i":277,"j":284,"loc":"Oration VIII · A Consolation upon the Departure of Sallust (2 of 2)","title":"Consolation through mind and God; farewell prayers","summary":"In a speech given to Pericles, Julian argues that a citizen of the world must obey his country, and that the mind, through memory, reason and imagination, can see an absent friend and commune with him and with God beyond the senses. Speaking in his own person, he takes comfort that no one can take his thoughts and that God guides those who trust in him, as Socrates' sign and Athene's counsel to Achilles show; he faults Odysseus for weeping on Calypso's island and holds that God favours prudence, not beauty or strength. Unlike Alexander, who craved a Homer, he is content to have Sallust as his truthful herald. Noting that Sallust goes to Illyria, Thrace and the Greeks among whom Julian grew up, he praises him as worthy of the best Greeks and ends with prayers for his safe journey and return.","ideas":["The mind sees the absent and communes with God without the senses","God aids the wary and prudent, as he did Odysseus","Julian wants a truthful eyewitness, not a Homer, as his herald","The Greeks sought truth by reason rather than incredible fables"],"terms":[{"t":"nous (mind)","d":"The faculty by which, in Pericles' speech, friends behold each other across distance and approach the incorporeal."}]}]});